>  I tested this behaviour with different setups and
>different harddisks. I use a SCSI disk and a Compaq
>Controller and Kernel 2.4.18 with a Debian 3.0.
>
>  Any idea what is happening? If I just do a reboot,
>cold or via software everything is alright.

On the off chance that the obvious is not fully understood, you
should cleanly 'shutdown' linux to SRM (or AlphaBIOS, etc.)
before cutting power.

It's not entirely clear under what conditions the disk is
corrupted.  Is it okay if you 'shutdown' linux and reboot?  Is it
bad if you 'shutdown' linux, cut power after Linux is halted,
restore power, and boot?  If 'yes' to both questions, such that
power cycling happens only outside of Linux, that would be ugly.
In that case, could it be the BIOS firmware (SRM, AlphaBIOS,
etc.) that is causing the corruption?  Yikes!

Is SCSI parity enabled?  I'm not sure how to check that, but it
would not be good to run without parity checking on the cable.

Is the bus properly terminated?  When I was trying to hook up a
CDROM drive and burner to a UP2K, I had all sorts of trouble
until I finally followed the advice of Michal from Harddata to
use a separate (active) terminator unit instead of relying on a
terminator built-in to the last drive.  Also, of course, make
sure there is exactly one terminator (or drive with termination
enabled) and that it is located at the end of the cable.  If you
are using LVD, make sure you have a twisted-pair cable.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
rm.riches@verizon.net


