Just so anybody else will know,.. the Red Hat 7.2 installer for Alpha
does work for installing to RAID-1 devices (including putting / on
RAID).  And, the stock SMP kernel does have RAID-1 support for
autodetection.  The only trick that I encountered is that an additional
single hard drive is needed to hold the /boot partition.  This disk is
needed because aboot needs to be written to a disk with BSD style
partitions.  The installer will automatically write aboot to this disk
and configure it properly.  This config worked for me, after hours and
hours of trial and error.

--
Chris

Chris Szilagyi wrote:

> Hello,
>
> I am trying to install Red Hat 7.2 on an Alpha DP264, and have all
> partitions on RAID-1.  The installer allowed to set up RAID-1 but
> wouldn't boot from the SRM, I'm guessing because it used DOS style
> partitions for the RAID, which won't support aboot.  So I have
> installed a third independent drive which will hold the /boot
> partition as well as aboot.  After adding an entry to /etc/aboot.conf
> like this:
>
> 8:1/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32.5smp root=/dev/md0 md=/dev/sda1,/dev/sdb1
>
