Jim Richardson wrote:

>  I am told that the mobo is a "PC164". It has 2 ide channels, a mix of
> 32 bit and 64 bit PCI cards, ISA and ps/2 mouse/kbd.
>  I am setting it up with case/ps/drives/etc and wondered about PCI
> graphics cards. Will the Alpha use standard Intel PCI cards or is it
> different like the Mac PCI?
> 

For linux, look at the distribution's hardware-specific pages. A PC164
is actually quite verstile wr.t. grafics cards. The 'blessed' cards
include old Trio64 (not Trio64V or '+' , just plain), ELSA Gloria
Synergy 8, ATI Mach64 (some), which are supported also under VMS and
(partially) Tru64. For Linux, I've heard about Matrox Millenium.

Note: Unless you got the board with RAM, it is probably quite expensive.
You need at least 4 * 16MB PS/2 SIMMs, Fast Page ECC (36bit), which are
not that widespread. 

Network cards should not be that problematic under Linux, I hope.

There is an additional twist with the power supply: It is ATX with 3.3V,
but the board does NOT have the starter logic to provide a Power On
signal to the ATX connector. You can bridge the pin (look for 'ATX and
connector pins' or such, there are some pictures in the net) to GND,
provided you have separate mains switch at the rear. Highly recommended
anyhow. Professional do not trust flaky soft-start PS.

If you have to buy the SIMMs, I'd think about it twice. It might be
easier to keep the CPU, sell the board and try to get a LX or even SX
board instead, which uses DIMMs.

Documentation can be found at 
http://www.support.compaq.com/alpha-tools/documentation/current/chip-docs.html

SRM can be found with installation notes at:
ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/v6.2/alphapc164

But note that the SRM for PC164 has a slight bug w.r.t. to the
configuration of ISA cards. If you are unable to permanently set
boot_action and bootdef_device (?), drop me a mail - I've found a trick
to cirumvent this.

