Hi again.

Ummm... I do boot from the Promise. The only catch is you have to flash your
motherboard with a milo image compiled versus a kernel supporting the U66.
Of course, the kernel code must be tweaked so as not to tweak the U66... :)
Actually, that is imho the greater problem of this card; once you set it up,
there is no way you can reset it (in software)... What i want to say is
that, during the initialization of the controller as done by the pdc-202xx
driver, the u66 is supposed to be in a 'reset' state and then tuned to the
speed of the faster drive attached to it (for every channel, of course),
independent of whether you have set CONFIG_PDC202XX_BURST (that will merely
allow the controller to go beyond pio4). However, there is no means of
getting the controller back to its initial state, unless (of course) if you
cold-reset your system. The trick here is to build a kernel with pdc support
but with the 'tuning' phase left off the scene (i guess you wouldn't mind
booting off a snail-speed drive, if it's actually attached to the pdc... I
mean, the only thing you have to wait for is for milo to load the
kernel...). After that, the loaded kernel can slice and dice and do anything
he deems proper with the U66 without any problem... I could send you the
milo image i use, although i bear no responsibility for whatever might come
out of it... ;)

Bye,
Nick.

