alan@rmdir.fay.demon.co.uk wrote:
> 
> I'm trying to install RedHat 7.2 on a very fast Alpha
> yes all 3 (4) CD's.

Apparently you already installed and now you are trying to boot.
Right?  You are doing that in a too complicated way.

> boot_osflags            root=/dev/sda3 console=ttyS0

This is plain wrong!  You are trying to boot a SCSI device using
a kernel 2.4.9-32.5 which has SCSI support as modules and there
is no reference to initrd.

Most likely setting 'boot_osflags' to '0' will be enough, if an
installation was correct, although you may not see your console
messages until you will get 'login:' prompt.  Check later
/etc/aboot.conf and pick a right line or edit one more to your liking.

Alternatively boot with '-fl i', review "canned" boot commands from
'aboot> ' prompt and type explicitely what you need following examples
displayed on your screen after you typed 'l'.

If you truly do not have initrd (a one should have been created
for you in an installation process but maybe something went wrong)
then you can always boot from your installation CD, switch to a text
console, get to your disk and create required initrd image using
'mkinitrd' utility (a shell script - really).

  Michal


