M�ns Rullg�rd wrote:

> I don't see why it should break.

One reason usually is usually responsible for all compatibility problems: 
pointer-integer conversions. Pointers are 64bit on Alphas. E.g. if there 
are any fixed structures with fields used for integers and pointers at 
different occasions, this will break, as they're trying to cast 64bit 
pointers to 32 bit integers. If these structures are used for file saving, 
you've got an even more serious problem. Shouldn't be a problem with 
OpenOffice, as this is using XML as file format, but you never know. Of 
course such code is so dirty you should better call it broken, but it's 
that coding style that prevents 64bit Netscape versions before the era of 
Mozilla and that made Digital provide a simulated 32bit environment for 
applications that depend on it (like netscape).

I don't think the only problem with the build is a different location of a 
Java VM, otherwise someone would have made a build already...

Andreas.



